              text: "# Climate Risk Assessment: 2025 Data Analysis\n\n## Executive Summary\nThe data presents a **mixed risk profile** — measurable progress coexisting with accelerating systemic threats.\n\n---\n\n## Risk Register\n\n| Risk Factor | Severity | Likelihood | Risk Level |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1.3°C warming trajectory | High | Confirmed | 🔴 Critical |\n| Arctic ice loss | High | Confirmed | 🔴 Critical |\n| CO2 at 425ppm | High | Confirmed | 🔴 Critical |\n| 2% emissions reduction pace | High | Likely | 🟠 High |\n| Renewable transition gap | Medium | Likely | 🟡 Moderate |\n\n---\n\n## Detailed Findings\n\n### 🔴 Critical Concerns\n\n**Temperature Trajectory**\n- 1.3°C leaves only **0.2°C buffer** before the 1.5°C Paris threshold\n- Rate of increase suggests that threshold is **realistically imminent**\n- Feedback loops at this level become increasingly difficult to reverse\n\n**CO2 Concentration**\n- 425ppm **exceeds safe zone estimates** (~350ppm)\n- Atmospheric CO2 is a *lagging indicator* — warming effects are already locked in beyond current readings\n\n**Arctic Ice Loss**\n- Record lows signal potential **albedo feedback acceleration**\n- Disrupts global weather patterns and ocean circulation\n- Largely **irreversible** on human timescales\n\n---\n\n### 🟠 High Risk\n\n**Insufficient Emissions Reduction Rate**\n- 2% annual reduction is **far below** the ~7-10% annually required to meet 1.5°C targets\n- Progress exists but represents a **critical pace mismatch**\n\n---\n\n### 🟡 Moderate / Positive Signals\n\n**Renewable Energy Growth**\n- 35% of electricity is meaningful progress\n- 6-point increase in ~2 years suggests **accelerating momentum**\n- However, electricity is only ~20% of total energy use globally\n\n---\n\n## Key Uncertainties & Data Limitations\n\n- ⚠️ No data on methane or other greenhouse gases\n- ⚠️ Regional variation not captured\n- ⚠️ No land use or deforestation data included\n- ⚠️ Electricity share ≠ total energy decarbonization\n\n---\n\n## Overall Assessment\n\n> **The data describes a system under serious stress with insufficient corrective response speed.** Progress is real but structurally inadequate. The gap between current trajectory and safe outcomes is widening, not closing.\n\n### Priority Recommendations\n1. **Treat pace of transition as the primary risk variable** — direction is correct, speed is not\n2. **Monitor Arctic indicators closely** as early warning signals\n3. **Expand data collection** beyond electricity to total energy, land use, and non-CO2 emissions\n4. **Avoid complacency from positive trends** — 35% renewables and 2% emissions cuts are insufficient at current CO2 levels",
